- On Intentionalism, point graph (source: source.txt)
    Summary: The Gale-Shapley structure gives proposers systematically better outcomes, so most people stay receivers by default, and intention must be practiced to switch sides. (¶1, ¶2, ¶9, ¶56)
        - 1 Some people consistently end up with better outcomes for reasons having nothing to do with talent, luck, or even the quality of their choices. (¶2)
            - 1.1 It is about whether they propose or receive. (¶3)
            - 1.2 In 1962, David Gale and Lloyd Shapley worked on creating stable pairings, where no two unmatched people would prefer each other over their assigned partners. (¶4)
            - 1.3 They proved you could always match everyone this way, and presented an algorithm to find such matchings. (¶5)
            - 1.4 In their deferred acceptance algorithm, one side makes proposals in order of preference, and the other accepts or tentatively rejects. (¶6)
            - 1.5 Evidence: Proposers get their best possible partner among all stable matchings, while receivers get their worst possible partner. (¶6)
            - 1.6 This is not slightly better or worse: it is systematically, provably, mathematically extreme for each individual. (¶7)
        - 2 Theme: Real-world situations share the proposer-receiver structure, even roughly, so the same pattern should appear. (¶8, ¶9)
            - 2.1 When systems have this structure, proposers systematically get better outcomes than receivers. (¶9)
            - 2.2 The situations are not running the exact Gale-Shapley algorithm, but they share the structure: one side initiates, the other responds. (¶8)
        - 3 Theme: Four everyday choices show the default: deciding what you want versus choosing among what is offered. (¶10)
            - 3.1 Example: Netflix: scrolling through recommendations until something looks good enough, rather than deciding what kind of experience you wanted. (¶11)
            - 3.2 Example: Phone: choosing among what was prominently displayed, rather than researching what features you actually needed. (¶12)
            - 3.3 Example: Job: applying to positions that happened to be posted, rather than pursuing companies doing work you cared about. (¶13)
            - 3.4 Example: Friendship: accepting a connection that formed through proximity or circumstance, rather than seeking out someone with qualities you value. (¶14)
            - 3.5 For most of these, most of us are receivers: choosing from what is presented, not pursuing what we want intentionally. (¶15)
            - 3.6 The insidious thing is not terrible outcomes; receivers get acceptable, good enough ones: a decent show, a functional phone, a reasonable job, pleasant friendships. (¶16)
            - 3.7 Nuance: But decent is not the same as what would actually serve you best. (¶17)
            - 3.8 Outcomes tend to favor whoever is doing the proposing, not because of a conspiracy but because the structure itself creates the advantage. (¶18)
            - 3.9 The system is self-sustaining precisely because it does not screw you badly enough to make you revolt. (¶19)
                - 3.9.1 It just quietly, consistently ensures you get less than you could have if you were on the other side of the equation. (¶19)
            - 3.10 You do not even know what you are missing, because you have never practiced wanting something specific enough to pursue it. (¶20)
        - 4 Theme: You need intention to propose, but you develop intention through proposing. (¶21, ¶23)
            - 4.1 To be a proposer you need to know what you want, and someone who has spent a whole life as a receiver would not know. (¶21)
            - 4.2 You cannot develop clear, intentional preferences by choosing among presented options; those choices reveal which option you dislike least. (¶22)
            - 4.3 This is the trap: you need intention to propose, but you develop intention through proposing. (¶23)
            - 4.4 Example: The music you listen to: are those your favorite songs, or just the best matches from what the algorithm served you? (¶24)
            - 4.5 Example: The person you are dating: did you seek someone compatible, or swipe through available profiles and pick the most appealing match? (¶25)
            - 4.6 After years of receiver mode, most people's preferences are just reactions to what gets put in front of them. (¶26)
            - 4.7 Nuance: They have lost, or never developed, the capacity to want something independent of what is being offered. (¶26)
        - 5 Theme: Intention and initiative build progressively, like a muscle. (¶27)
            - 5.1 You cannot jump straight from picking from Netflix recommendations to a clear vision for your life's work. (¶27)
            - 5.2 Example: Start tonight: before opening any app, decide what you actually want from the next hour, specifically whether to laugh, learn, feel calm, or be challenged. (¶28)
            - 5.3 Then pursue that intentionally: do not open Netflix and see what it suggests, do not scroll TikTok until something catches you. (¶29)
            - 5.4 The practice is not about one evening; it rebuilds the capacity to intend and then act on that intention. (¶30)
            - 5.5 Do it enough times and patterns in what satisfies you become preferences, and preferences become values. (¶30)
            - 5.6 You cannot think your way into values; you have to practice your way into them. (¶31)
        - 6 Theme: The same receiver default shows up in major life decisions. (¶32)
            - 6.1 Example: A last major life decision: getting married, taking a job, moving to a city, or choosing what to study. (¶32)
            - 6.2 The question is whether you decided what you wanted first and then pursued it, or evaluated options that presented themselves. (¶33)
            - 6.3 Most people, most of the time, are evaluating presented options. (¶34)
                - 6.3.1 This feels like agency because you are choosing, but you are choosing as a receiver, in a structure that systematically favors the other side. (¶34)
            - 6.4 Proposers decide intentionally what kind of work they want and pursue those specific opportunities. (¶35)
            - 6.5 They identify what they value in a partner and seek those people out. (¶35)
                - 6.5.1 They pick cities based on how they want to live rather than where they got offers. (¶35)
            - 6.6 Those people end up with systematically better outcomes. (¶35)
            - 6.7 Not because they are smarter or luckier, but because they are on the other side of a structure that mathematically favors proposers. (¶36)
        - 7 Theme: When most people are receivers, entire systems shift to optimize for whoever is doing the proposing. (¶37)
            - 7.1 Companies build for what they can convince people to want among presented options, not for what people need. (¶38)
            - 7.2 Dating apps optimize for engagement among available profiles, not for people finding compatible partners. (¶38)
            - 7.3 Content platforms test what keeps you scrolling among suggested posts, not what would genuinely satisfy you. (¶38)
            - 7.4 The proposers in these systems are not the users; they are the platforms. (¶39)
            - 7.5 The platforms get your attention, data, and money, while you get outcomes good enough to keep participating but not serving your best interests. (¶39)
            - 7.6 You stay because leaving feels harder than accepting good enough; that stability, resistance to change, is why the system persists. (¶40)
        - 8 Theme: Understanding the disadvantage does not remove it; you have to actually switch positions. (¶41)
            - 8.1 This is harder than it sounds, because the environment is designed to keep us receiving. (¶42)
                - 8.1.1 Every interface, recommendation system, application process, and social script presents options and waits for you to choose. (¶42)
            - 8.2 Proposing means deciding intentionally what you want before seeing what is available. (¶43)
            - 8.3 It means pursuing specific things rather than evaluating presented options. (¶43)
            - 8.4 It means being willing to create opportunities rather than waiting for them. (¶43)
            - 8.5 It means accepting the friction of going against default flows. (¶43)
            - 8.6 It means asking what do I actually want before every major decision and most minor ones. (¶44)
                - 8.6.1 Then acting on that answer intentionally, even when it is inconvenient. (¶44)
            - 8.7 Most people will not do this: it is effortful, and the receiver position is comfortable enough not to motivate change. (¶45)
            - 8.8 After years of receiver mode, most people have lost the capacity to even know what they would want to propose. (¶45)
            - 8.9 For those who do shift, real life does not follow the exact mathematics but shares enough of the structure. (¶46)
            - 8.10 Systematically better, not just incrementally: structurally. (¶46)
            - 8.11 The question is not whether this dynamic exists; the mathematics and the patterns we see around us both point to it. (¶47)
            - 8.12 The question is whether you are willing to do something about it. (¶48)
        - 9 Theme: Start with tomorrow morning, not your career or life partner. (¶49)
            - 9.1 Before you check your phone, decide intentionally what you actually want from the first hour: calm, energy, connection, or solitude. (¶50)
            - 9.2 If you want calm, do not open apps that will agitate you; if connection, reach out to someone specific; if energy, do something physical. (¶51)
            - 9.3 Notice how different this feels from scrolling until something grabs you. (¶52)
            - 9.4 Do this for a week with small things, breakfast, evening activities, weekend plans, noticing what actually satisfies you versus what just filled time. (¶53)
            - 9.5 Those patterns are the beginning of intention. (¶53)
            - 9.6 Then scale up: before looking at job postings, decide what kind of work you want to do. (¶54)
            - 9.7 Before swiping on dating apps, decide what kind of relationship you are looking for, and before browsing products, what problem you are solving. (¶54)
            - 9.8 The practice builds the muscle, the muscle enables larger intentions, larger intentions become values, and values make you a proposer. (¶55)
            - 9.9 Proposers, whether in the pure mathematics of matching algorithms or the messy approximations of real life, end up with better outcomes. (¶56)
            - 9.10 Not because they are special, but because they are on the other side of the equation. (¶57)
        - 10 Theme: The appendix gives concrete exercises for practicing intention in progressively larger domains. (¶58)
            - 10.1 Theme: Entertainment: name, seek, and protect your intention over three weeks. (¶59)
                - 10.1.1 Week 1: before opening any entertainment app, write down or say aloud what you want to feel. (¶59)
                    - 10.1.1.1 If you cannot name it, close the app and try again later. (¶59)
                    - 10.1.1.2 Keep a simple log: intention, what you watched, and whether it delivered. (¶59)
                    - 10.1.1.3 Example: I want to laugh, or learn about X, or feel inspired, or relax completely. (¶59)
                - 10.1.2 Week 2: based on your named intention, search for specific content rather than scrolling recommendations. (¶60)
                    - 10.1.2.1 Example: If the goal is to laugh, search for specific comedians you know make you laugh. (¶60)
                    - 10.1.2.2 Notice how much faster you find satisfaction. (¶60)
                    - 10.1.2.3 Example: If the goal is to learn, search specific topics, not just what is trending. (¶60)
                - 10.1.3 Week 3: set a timer for how long you intended to spend. (¶61)
                    - 10.1.3.1 When autoplay starts, ask whether this serves your original intention. (¶61)
                    - 10.1.3.2 Practice closing the app when your intention is fulfilled, not when you are exhausted. (¶61)
            - 10.2 Theme: Shopping: define the problem, list must-haves, research intentionally, ignore the store layout, and wait a day over $100. (¶62)
                - 10.2.1 Define the problem specifically: not I need a phone, but reliable video calls with family. (¶62)
                    - 10.2.1.1 A good camera for travel, and battery that lasts full days. (¶62)
                - 10.2.2 List the 3-5 features that actually matter, being clear about what you will use versus what sounds cool. (¶62)
                - 10.2.3 Research those specific features, not best phones 2025. (¶62)
                - 10.2.4 In person, go directly to compare items that meet your criteria; do not browse the store layout. (¶62)
                - 10.2.5 For anything over $100, wait a day after deciding; if you still want that specific item, buy it. (¶62)
                - 10.2.6 Example: Toothpaste run: define what you actually need, sensitive teeth, whitening, or just clean teeth. (¶63)
                    - 10.2.6.1 Research which ingredient addresses that need, buy it, and notice the difference from comparing packaging. (¶63)
            - 10.3 Theme: Jobs and career: quarterly intention-setting, even if you are not job hunting. (¶64)
                - 10.3.1 Map your energy for two weeks, noting which tasks give you energy and which drain you. (¶64)
                - 10.3.2 Identify patterns: what type of problems do you enjoy solving, and what environments let you thrive? (¶64)
                - 10.3.3 Write 5 non-negotiables for your next role, specific ones about problem type, people, and environment, not good pay. (¶64)
                - 10.3.4 Research companies doing that work regardless of whether they have postings, contact people there, and ask about their experience. (¶64)
                - 10.3.5 If you must apply to postings, apply only to roles that match at least 4 of your 5 criteria, and customize every application. (¶65)
                - 10.3.6 Track whether you are submitting many applications as a receiver or carefully selecting few as a proposer. (¶65)
            - 10.4 Theme: Relationships: write your intention, identify values, and seek people who demonstrate them. (¶66)
                - 10.4.1 Write what kind of relationship you want to build: what daily life looks like, how conflict is handled, what you build together. (¶66)
                - 10.4.2 Identify the 3-5 values that absolutely must align, specific things like prioritizing growth or wanting intellectual partnership. (¶66)
                - 10.4.3 Seek people who demonstrate those values, not just attractive profiles. (¶66)
                - 10.4.4 Use the first date to ask questions that reveal value alignment, not surface chat. (¶66)
                - 10.4.5 For friendships, name what you need: friends who push you intellectually, friends you can be quiet with, friends who share this specific hobby. (¶67)
                - 10.4.6 Go where those people are: join specific communities, not just networking events. (¶67)
                - 10.4.7 Initiate based on intention: invite people to specific things you actually want to do, not a generic let us hang out sometime. (¶67)
            - 10.5 Theme: Daily micro-practices: a morning routine, an evening review, and the pause practice. (¶68)
                - 10.5.1 Before checking your phone, write down your three intentions for today. (¶68)
                    - 10.5.1.1 Not be productive but specific: have a meaningful conversation with X, make progress on Y, spend 30 minutes on Z. (¶68)
                - 10.5.2 Evening review: did you act as proposer or receiver today, and what would you propose instead tomorrow? (¶69)
                    - 10.5.2.1 Note one small thing you will do intentionally tomorrow. (¶69)
                - 10.5.3 The pause practice: before any significant choice today, pause for 10 seconds. (¶70)
                    - 10.5.3.1 This builds awareness, which is the first step. (¶70)
                    - 10.5.3.2 Ask: am I choosing this intentionally, or just selecting from what is in front of me? (¶70)
            - 10.6 Theme: Tracking progress: keep a journal with three columns, situation, mode, and outcome. (¶71)
                - 10.6.1 Situation: what was the choice? (¶71)
                - 10.6.2 Mode: did I propose, deciding what I want and then pursuing it, or receive, choosing from what was offered? (¶71)
                - 10.6.3 Outcome: how satisfied am I with the result? (¶71)
                - 10.6.4 After 30 days the pattern is clear: proposer entries show higher satisfaction, and this evidence motivates continued practice. (¶72)
            - 10.7 The key insight: intention is not about always knowing exactly what you want for your entire life. (¶73)
            - 10.8 It is about practicing the question what do I want before someone else provides the answer. (¶73)
            - 10.9 Start small: the muscle builds, the capacity grows, and eventually the intentional approach becomes natural. (¶73)
